      "text": "Let's use algebra to solve this problem. Let \\( b \\) represent the weight of one bowling ball in pounds, and let \\( c \\) represent the weight of one canoe in pounds. According to the problem, we have two pieces of information that can be translated into equations:\n\n1. Seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes.\n   \\[\n   7b = 3c\n   \\]\n\n2. Two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds.\n   \\[\n   2c = 56\n   \\]\n\nFirst, we solve the second equation for \\( c \\):\n\\[\n2c = 56\n\\]\n\\[\nc = \\frac{56}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 28\n\\]\nSo, each canoe weighs 28 pounds.\n\nNext, we substitute \\( c = 28 \\) into the first equation to find \\( b \\):\n\\[\n7b = 3c\n\\]\n\\[\n7b = 3 \\times 28\n\\]\n\\[\n7b = 84\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{84}{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 12\n\\]\nTherefore, the weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
